WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:07.749 align:start position:0%
 
hi there steve coffin here  you know one subject that comes up all  the time is you know if i learn these  languages what can i do with it you know  the relationship between languages and  work or career profession and so forth  um  okay i want to talk a bit about that  with reference to my own personal  experience  first of all a general statement i  am not aware of a lot of professions  where  you can say hire me because i speak a  lot of languages there are some  obviously if you're an interpreter a  translator a teacher of languages  then those things you know speaking i  speak a lot of languages might get to a  job in most cases what i found with  speaking languages was  that it increased  the opportunities that are going to come  your way  but you still had to have other things  working for you had to have other skills  uh knowledge of a specific you know  sector market an ability to do business  ability to be a  reliable energetic uh person in in any  number of fields  uh but in my own case for example  there's no question that  the fact that i left montreal as an  anglophone  went to france studied there for three  years and the last two was at lancaster  they did politique in paris syanspo and  then i wrote my  canadian diplomatic or foreign service  exam  in french as an anglophone  i am sure that that helped me  uh be selected into the canadian  diplomatic service because they want  people who are at least fluent  so here's a language we're lying here's  a profession where languages count they  want people who are fluent at the very  least in the two official languages so  if an anglophone wrote the  foreign service exam in french that  probably put me in a select group so i  had a better chance of being selected  that was the first thing  when i was in ottawa in my year in  training with the trade commissioner  service  and i heard that the government was  preparing to send someone to learn  chinese i started taking chinese lessons  on my own so that i could go to the  director of personnel and say i hear you  want to send someone to learn chinese  because canada is about to recognize the  people's republic of china i've already  started i just want you to know that so  the fact that that i have already  undertaken you know to start learning  chinese  makes them probably think why wouldn't  we choose him he's already motivated  so that helped  obviously learning chinese then i was  able to go into china help canadian  business people who were negotiating  with their chinese counterparts in  different situations at the canton trade  fair and so forth  i subsequently went to japan lived there  learned japanese  and because i  learned japanese quite quickly on my own  i should add  and i had a lot of contacts in the  forest product sector while working at  the embassy a canadian company that  wanted to set up their own office in  japan  they saw someone me although quite young  at the time who had contacts in the  industry and who spoke japanese so i was  given the job of setting up their  uh representative  or subsidiary in tokyo so  obviously my knowledge of japanese not  only enabled me to  communicate at various levels in the  japanese  lumber  trade sector and not just those  trading company people who spoke english  but a wide variety of people so i got  that job  i subsequently went back to japan for  another company  uh again because i spoke japanese and  had contacts  um  then uh  let's see i guess the next  major sort of language learning spurt  for me was 1987 i was between jobs  i had been hired by a company  that did business in europe and i said  i'm going to really learn up german so i  spent a month  scouring the second-hand bookstores in  vancouver finding books that had  text and and uh vocabulary lists  for each chapter because  in those days i mean i just didn't want  to look every word up in the dictionary  there were no online dictionaries there  was no link so i found a whole pile of  books  excellent books in german  and i found some good uh audio cassettes  for learning german so i did a lot of  listening and reading and learned of  german well it turned out that in the  1990s i did a fair amount of business in  germany and we were selling wood from  canada into germany and so i had  visitors from germany and i traveled in  germany and once you got past the the  main sort of  lumber agents a lot of the  consumers the wood processors the  different uh  customers for our products  were much more comfortable speaking  german than speaking english i think it  helped me do business there  thereafter  we started doing business in sweden  which became a big supplying country for  us  and so i again got after swedish which i  had some background in because i was  born there lived there for five years  forgot swedish but then  uh you know i spent a summer there as a  16 year old  but i decided i'm going to really learn  this language so i got all these audio  books and books and i've mentioned him  on lindquist you know swedish history  and stuff and i ended up doing a fair  amount of business and i think i had  better relations and  developed a better relationship of trust  with my suppliers because i spoke  swedish and when we had meetings and  they wanted me to explain the japanese  market to them in front of their  production people not all of whom were  so fluent in english even in sweden the  fact that i was able to do this and  field questions in swedish and explain  what the customer's requirements were  and the market and how it was structured  all of this in swedish definitely helped  um  those are the main areas where my  languages have helped me i mean  when i set up my own company we did some  business in spain  and i was able to contact people by the  phone and we had some spanish customers  come through and  being able to speak spanish certainly  helped we have a very good customer in  france with whom i speak french all the  time exclusively  um you know my business once i set it up  was primarily marketing to japan so that  i guess the biggest payback was my my  japanese  japanese  language skills which helped me develop  a market position in japan  you know i i think  in business you have to be lucky and you  have to seize opportunities that come  your way the more languages you speak  the more opportunities you're going to  have  but you still have to be  committed sincere hard-working  you have sometimes take chances you  don't want to take ridiculous chances  so you have to have all those other  things  and luck of course to be successful  about the languages just increase  the number of opportunities that are  going to come your way  it increases your opportunity to connect  with people you can connect with more  people you can understand people better  so all of those things are the benefits  of learning to speak a number of  languages and you never know which  languages are going to come in handy  when so i hope that's of  some interest to those of you who  frequently ask you know what are the  benefits of learning other languages  bye for now  you

